  "query_preview": "..Currently, a weak mid-level trough is traversing east \nacross the Great Basin into the Southwest. Stronger ascent remains \nfurther south across AZ, keeping most of UT and southwest WY dry and \ncool. A light dusting of mountain snow looks to have occurred across \nour southern UT mountains, though dry air and lack of meaningful \nascent has kept any precipitation confined much further south. \n\nTransient ridging looks to develop across the Great Basin following \nthis system with quiescent weather expected to persist through at \nleast mid-week. Temperatures are expected to warm slightly through \nmid-week, though will remain near-normal each day. Conditions are \nforecast to remain dry and stable with terrain driven winds as a \nresult. \n\nCome Thursday, a strong ridge of high pressure looks to develop \nacross the Eastern Pacific just off the coast of Baja California. \nAdditionally, numerous mid-level troughs look to eject east into CA. \nUnfortunately, a split flow pattern looks to develop as this ridge \nwill shift these troughs north initially, will result in these \ntroughs digging south across the Sierra range into Southern CA and \nAZ. This will yield weaker ascent across the forecast area and keep \ncolder mid-level temperatures further north resulting in less \nprecipitation occurring across the forecast area, if at all. This \npattern looks to persist through the end of November, keeping us dry \nfor the most part. Some perturbations look to split north through \nthe period yielding low PoPs across our northern UT high terrain \n(~30%) which could result in some light mountain snow and perhaps \nvalley rainfall in the absence of meaningful large scale ascent. \n\nAround the end of November into early December, ensemble guidance \ncontinues to hint at a more active pattern raising PoPs in the \nforecast CWA-wide. However, numerous caveats exist with this \nforecast given large model spread down the line. While frequent \ntroughing is expected, ensemble guidance continues to struggle with \ntrough placement and whether they dive south across the Sierra range \nor hold together further north. When looking at meteograms for \nsnowfall across the Cottonwoods, it becomes evident that the models \nare rather uncertain yielding extreme differences in snowfall \naccumulations, potentially as low as 1\" to 40\". When diving into \ncluster analysis, it appears that the southern track is favored at \nthis time with around 60% of all members supporting this solution. \nThe forecast is expected to change as the month comes to an end, and \nthis forecaster certainly hopes that the southern track does not \ncontinue to remain favored similar to last season.",
